\title{Compositional Memory as Algebraic Quotienting}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 20, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop an algebraic theory of memory for finite streams. An experience stream is a word in the free monoid on an alphabet, while a compositional memory is a monoid homomorphism into a representation monoid. We prove that every finite-state compositional memory over a nonempty alphabet is necessarily lossy: two distinct streams receive the same representation. The induced observational indistinguishability is a monoid congruence, and the streams mapped to the neutral representation form a submonoid. The first isomorphism principle identifies the quotient of all streams by observational indistinguishability with the reachable submonoid of representations. We also study targeted forgetting, a canonical filter that deletes selected symbols. Its quotient is isomorphic to the monoid of observable outputs, and it satisfies a universal factorization property: every compositional summary that respects its identifications factors uniquely through the quotient. Algorithms for collision detection, erasure testing, quotient exploration, and targeted filtering are supplied with complexity bounds. The results provide a common language for finite-state compression, event filtering, automata, privacy-preserving coarsening, and sequence representation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A sequential memory receives a history and returns a representation. In many applications the history is assembled from smaller histories, and its representation can likewise be assembled from the representations of those parts. This compositional law occurs in streaming computation, finite automata, event processing, symbolic filtering, and compressed sequence summaries. It suggests that the appropriate foundational object is not an arbitrary function but a homomorphism between monoids.

Let $A$ be an alphabet. The collection $\Astar$ of all finite words over $A$ carries concatenation as an associative operation and the empty word $\eps$ as its identity. It is the free monoid on $A$. Let $R$ be a representation monoid with identity $1_R$. A compositional memory is a map $M:\Astar\to R$ satisfying
\[
 M(uv)=M(u)M(v), \qquad M(\eps)=1_R.
\]
No commutativity, cancellation, or finiteness is assumed unless explicitly stated. Thus the framework includes order-sensitive and irreversible summaries.

The central theme is that information loss has algebraic form. Equality of memory states induces a congruence on streams. The quotient by that congruence is isomorphic to the reachable part of the representation monoid. A compositional memory can therefore be understood exactly as a quotient followed by an injective encoding of quotient classes.

Our main contributions are fourfold. We establish unavoidable collisions for finite representation spaces over any nonempty alphabet; identify the completely erased streams as a submonoid; prove an observable quotient theorem; and define targeted forgetting together with its universal factorization property. We then translate the structure into computational procedures and applications.

\section{Algebraic preliminaries}

\begin{definition}[Monoid]
A monoid is a set $S$ equipped with an associative binary operation and an identity $1_S$. Thus $(xy)z=x(yz)$ and $1_Sx=x1_S=x$ for all $x,y,z\in S$.
\end{definition}

For an alphabet $A$, the free monoid $\Astar$ is the set of finite sequences of elements of $A$. Multiplication is concatenation and the identity is $\eps$. A word of length $n$ is written $a_1\cdots a_n$. A monoid homomorphism $F:S\to T$ satisfies $F(1_S)=1_T$ and $F(xy)=F(x)F(y)$. Its image
\[
 \im(F)=\{F(x):x\in S\}
\]
is a submonoid of $T$.

\begin{definition}[Experience stream and compositional memory]
An experience stream over $A$ is an element of $\Astar$. A compositional memory with representation monoid $R$ is a monoid homomorphism $M:\Astar\to R$.
\end{definition}

If $u=a_1\cdots a_n$, compositionality gives
\[
 M(u)=M(a_1)M(a_2)\cdots M(a_n).
\]
The order of factors matters when $R$ is noncommutative.

\begin{definition}[Observational indistinguishability]
For a compositional memory $M$, define
\[
 u\sim_M v \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad M(u)=M(v).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Congruence property]
The relation $\sim_M$ is an equivalence relation compatible with concatenation. More precisely, if $u\sim_M v$ and $x\sim_M y$, then $ux\sim_M vy$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity follow from equality in $R$. The hypotheses give $M(u)=M(v)$ and $M(x)=M(y)$, whence
\[
 M(ux)=M(u)M(x)=M(v)M(y)=M(vy).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Erased streams]
The erased-stream set of $M$ is
\[
 E_M=\{u\in\Astar:M(u)=1_R\}.
\]
These streams have the same representation as the empty stream.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Observational quotient]
The quotient $\Astar/{\sim_M}$ is the set of equivalence classes $[u]$, with multiplication $[u][v]=[uv]$ and identity $[\eps]$. The congruence property makes this operation independent of representatives.
\end{definition}

\section{Finite memory necessarily loses information}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-Memory Loss Theorem]
Let $A$ be nonempty, let $R$ be a finite monoid, and let $M:\Astar\to R$ be a compositional memory. Then there exist distinct streams $u,v\in\Astar$ such that $M(u)=M(v)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose $a\in A$. The words $\eps,a,a^2,a^3,\ldots$ are pairwise distinct, so $\Astar$ is infinite. A function from an infinite set to the finite set $R$ cannot be injective. Hence two distinct streams have the same image.
\end{proof}

The cardinality argument does not itself require compositionality; compositionality describes the structure propagated by collisions. Nor does the alphabet need to be finite. A single symbol already generates infinitely many words.

\begin{corollary}[Nontrivial indistinguishability]
Under the preceding hypotheses, at least one equivalence class of $\sim_M$ contains two distinct streams. Equivalently, observational indistinguishability is not equality.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The two streams supplied by the theorem are distinct and have equal images, hence occupy one class.
\end{proof}

A quantitative unary version gives finite certificates.

\begin{proposition}[Bounded unary collision certificate]
If $|R|=m$ and $A$ is nonempty, then for every $a\in A$ there exist integers $0\leq i<j\leq m$ such that
\[
 M(a^i)=M(a^j).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the finite pigeonhole principle to the $m+1$ values $M(a^0),\ldots,M(a^m)$.
\end{proof}

Equality at indices $i<j$ propagates: for every $t\geq0$,
\[
 M(a^{i+t})=M(a^i)M(a^t)=M(a^j)M(a^t)=M(a^{j+t}).
\]
The remembered trajectory of a repeated experience is therefore eventually periodic.

\section{Erasure and the observable quotient}

\begin{theorem}[Erased-Stream Submonoid Theorem]
For every compositional memory $M:\Astar\to R$, the erased-stream set $E_M$ is a submonoid. Explicitly, $\eps\in E_M$, and $u,v\in E_M$ implies $uv\in E_M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $M(\eps)=1_R$, the empty word belongs to $E_M$. If $M(u)=M(v)=1_R$, then
\[
 M(uv)=M(u)M(v)=1_R1_R=1_R,
\]
so $uv\in E_M$.
\end{proof}

In a general monoid, $E_M$ need not be closed under arbitrary insertion contexts, inverses need not exist, and a product can map to $1_R$ even when neither factor does. The guaranteed structure is exactly that of a submonoid.

\begin{theorem}[Observable Quotient Theorem]
Let $M:\Astar\to R$ be a compositional memory. Then
\[
 \Astar/{\sim_M}\;\cong\;\im(M)
\]
as monoids. The isomorphism is $\Phi([u])=M(u)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $[u]=[v]$, then $M(u)=M(v)$, so $\Phi$ is well defined. It preserves products since
\[
 \Phi([u][v])=M(uv)=M(u)M(v)=\Phi([u])\Phi([v]).
\]
It is surjective by the definition of the image. If $\Phi([u])=\Phi([v])$, then $u\sim_M v$, hence $[u]=[v]$; thus it is injective.
\end{proof}

Let $q:\Astar\to\Astar/{\sim_M}$ be the quotient map and let $\iota:\im(M)\hookrightarrow R$ be inclusion. The theorem yields the exact normal form
\[
 M=\iota\circ\Phi\circ q.
\]
The information loss occurs in $q$; the remaining maps are injective onto the reachable range.

\begin{corollary}[Number of observable classes]
If $R$ is finite, the number of observational classes equals $|\im(M)|$ and is at most $|R|$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Isomorphic finite sets have equal cardinality, and the image is a subset of $R$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Criterion for losslessness]
A compositional memory is lossless on all finite streams if and only if every class of $\sim_M$ is a singleton, equivalently if and only if the quotient map is injective.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each condition restates injectivity of $M$: equality of memory values must imply equality of words.
\end{proof}

No finite $R$ can satisfy this criterion over a nonempty alphabet.

\section{Targeted forgetting}

Let a retention policy be a function $r:A\to\{0,1\}$, where $r(a)=1$ means retain $a$ and $r(a)=0$ means erase it.

\begin{definition}[Targeted-forgetting map]
Define $T_r:\Astar\to\Astar$ on letters by
\[
 T_r(a)=
 \begin{cases}
 a,&r(a)=1,\\
 \eps,&r(a)=0,
 \end{cases}
\]
and extend multiplicatively. Thus $T_r(a_1\cdots a_n)=T_r(a_1)\cdots T_r(a_n)$. Operationally, this deletes every unretained symbol while preserving the relative order of retained symbols.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Compositionality]
For all $u,v\in\Astar$,
\[
 T_r(uv)=T_r(u)T_r(v),\qquad T_r(\eps)=\eps.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Splitting the list of letters at the boundary between $u$ and $v$ splits the concatenation of their images. The empty concatenation is $\eps$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Forgotten Symbols Are Erased]
If $r(a)=0$, then $a\in E_{T_r}$; equivalently, $T_r(a)=\eps$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
This is the erased branch of the definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Concrete range]
The image of $T_r$ consists exactly of words all of whose letters satisfy $r(a)=1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Filtering outputs only retained letters. Conversely, if every letter of $w$ is retained, then $T_r(w)=w$.
\end{proof}

In particular, $T_r$ is idempotent: $T_r(T_r(u))=T_r(u)$. It is a projection onto the submonoid of retained words.

\begin{theorem}[Universal Factorization Theorem for Targeted Forgetting]
Let $S$ be a monoid and let $G:\Astar\to S$ be a monoid homomorphism. Assume that for all $u,v$,
\[
 T_r(u)=T_r(v)\implies G(u)=G(v).
\]
If $q_r:\Astar\to\Astar/{\sim_{T_r}}$ is the quotient map, then there exists a unique monoid homomorphism
\[
 \overline{G}:\Astar/{\sim_{T_r}}\to S
\]
such that $\overline{G}\circ q_r=G$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Define $\overline{G}([u])=G(u)$. The assumption makes this independent of the representative. It preserves multiplication because $G$ does, and the factorization equation follows directly. If $H$ is another factorization, then for every class $[u]$,
\[
 H([u])=H(q_r(u))=G(u)=\overline{G}([u]),
\]
so $H=\overline{G}$.
\end{proof}

The hypothesis permits $G$ to discard additional distinctions but forbids it from recovering any distinction destroyed by $T_r$.

\begin{theorem}[Targeted-Forgetting Quotient Theorem]
The quotient induced by targeted forgetting satisfies
\[
 \Astar/{\sim_{T_r}}\;\cong\;\im(T_r),
\]
and the image is precisely the free monoid of words formed from retained symbols.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the Observable Quotient Theorem to $T_r$ and then use the concrete range proposition.
\end{proof}

Thus retained words form a canonical interface through which every compatible downstream compositional computation factors uniquely.

\section{Algorithms and computational illustrations}

\subsection{Streaming targeted forgetting}
Given a word of length $n$, scan left to right and append a symbol precisely when the policy retains it. After processing the first $i$ symbols, the output equals $T_r$ of that prefix. This invariant is immediate initially and is preserved by either appending a retained symbol or ignoring an erased one. The running time is $O(n)$ under constant-time policy lookup, and output storage is $O(k)$ for $k$ retained symbols. A streaming sink uses only $O(1)$ auxiliary space.

\subsection{Unary collision search}
For a finite monoid of size $m$ and a letter $a$, compute
\[
 1_R,M(a),M(a)^2,\ldots,M(a)^m.
\]
Store the first index of every state. The first repeated state gives $i<j\leq m$ with $M(a^i)=M(a^j)$. Correctness follows from the bounded unary collision proposition. With constant-time multiplication, equality, and hashing, time and space are $O(m)$.

\subsection{Finite quotient exploration}
When $A$ and the reachable range are finite, breadth-first search enumerates observable classes. Begin at $1_R$. For each discovered state $s$ and letter $a$, compute $sM(a)$ and enqueue it if unseen. Each reached state represents one quotient class by the Observable Quotient Theorem. If $q=|\im(M)|$ and $d=|A|$, the algorithm performs at most $qd$ transitions, taking $O(qd)$ time and $O(q)$ space under constant-time state operations.

\begin{example}[Parity memory]
Let $A=\{a,b\}$ and $R=\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ under addition. Assign $a\mapsto1$ and $b\mapsto0$. Then $M(u)$ records the parity of the number of occurrences of $a$. Distinct words collide when they have the same parity. The erased set consists of words containing an even number of $a$ symbols, and the quotient has exactly two classes.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Selective event logging]
Let $A=\{\mathrm{INFO},\mathrm{WARN},\mathrm{ERROR}\}$ and retain only WARN and ERROR. Then
\[
 T_r(\mathrm{INFO\ WARN\ INFO\ ERROR\ WARN})
 =\mathrm{WARN\ ERROR\ WARN}.
\]
Two logs are indistinguishable exactly when deleting INFO entries yields the same retained log. Every compositional alerting system insensitive to INFO factors uniquely through the filtered logs.
\end{example}

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Finite automata and streaming summaries.}
A deterministic finite-state stream processor often updates a state after each symbol. When summaries combine associatively, as with transition transformations or syntactic monoids, it fits the present model. Finite-memory loss guarantees collisions, while the quotient theorem identifies states with observational classes of histories. Quotient exploration constructs the reachable transition graph.

\paragraph{Event filtering and logging.}
Targeted forgetting models policies that retain selected event types. If downstream modules are insensitive to dropped events, raw logs may be replaced by filtered logs without changing their outputs. The uniqueness clause determines the induced operations on the filtered interface.

\paragraph{Privacy-preserving coarsening.}
A privacy transformation removes distinctions between records or event sequences. In a compositional setting, the quotient describes exactly the information still available. A downstream map respecting the privacy congruence cannot distinguish records that coarsening has identified. This structural non-recovery statement is not, by itself, a probabilistic privacy guarantee.

\paragraph{Representation learning.}
Sequence embeddings map many inputs to one representation. When an embedding has an associative composition law, the quotient viewpoint separates which streams are identified from how their classes are encoded. The reachable embedding algebra is determined up to isomorphism by its observational congruence.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

The framework makes few assumptions and hence gives structural rather than semantic conclusions. Finiteness forces a collision but does not measure its importance. Application-specific loss functions are needed to distinguish harmless from consequential identifications.

Exact compositionality is also a substantive assumption. Context-dependent updates, approximate equality, stochastic transitions, and bounded windows may require metric quotients, Markov kernels, monoid actions, categories, or transducers. The erased-stream submonoid records only the identity class; a memory may lose distinctions without sending either stream to the identity, so the full congruence remains the complete invariant.

Finally, finiteness does not imply computational efficiency. A finite monoid may be enormous and multiplication expensive. The structural theorems are representation-independent, whereas the stated algorithmic bounds assume explicit states and a cost model.

\section{Future work}

Natural extensions include classifying congruences on a free monoid that admit small finite quotients, optimizing retention policies under state budgets, and comparing memories by refinement. If $\sim_{M_1}$ is contained in $\sim_{M_2}$, then $M_1$ preserves at least as many distinctions as $M_2$, suggesting a partial order of information content.

Approximate variants may replace equality by a metric tolerance and ask when approximate indistinguishability is stable under concatenation. Probabilistic variants can quantify information while retaining the algebraic quotient as a zero-error backbone. Another direction is learning a finite congruence from observed equivalence judgments and reconstructing a minimal compositional representation.

Targeted forgetting also raises optimization problems: choose a retained subalphabet satisfying privacy or storage constraints while preserving a downstream task. The universal property guarantees correctness after compatibility is established; selecting the policy remains the substantive challenge.

\section{Conclusion}

Over a nonempty alphabet, every finite compositional memory identifies distinct streams. These identifications form a monoid congruence, completely erased histories form a submonoid, and the quotient by observational indistinguishability is isomorphic to the reachable representation algebra. Targeted forgetting gives an explicit symbol filter and a canonical quotient through which every compatible compositional summary factors uniquely.

The resulting picture is exact: a memory first collapses histories into classes and then represents those classes. Information loss becomes visible, compositional, and amenable to algorithmic exploration.

